A||||11||Brian84SE||emission failure update||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||02-19-2000||09:07 PM||||OK, for those who havent been watching my posts, let me go through this again:<BR>  84 4cyl. 85,000 miles<BR>First time through emissions, HC passed (barely) CO passed (good) NOx (High)<BR>Put on another EGR from the junkyard.<BR>2nd time: HC same, CO same, NOx was 200ppm lower. Consulted the list, you guys said try O2 sensor and/or catalytic converter.  Changed O2 sensor.<BR>3rd time: HC a bit lower, CO same, NOx back to orginal high (2100ppm, state max=1400) Any thoughts?? Will Catalytic make that big of a difference?  How hard is it going to be to change?  Thanx guys,<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||02-19-2000||09:12 PM||||I don't know all the hardware the 4-cyl has, but NOx is controlled mainly through the EGR system. Possible culprits, (I know you did some work with it, but don't want the possibility of over looking something), EGR tube, EGR valve, EGR solonoid(does the 4-cyl have one?)vaccume lines that operate the system. Like I said, EGR does the larger part of clearing up NOx, don't know how much of a role the cat plays.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian84SE||02-19-2000||10:18 PM||||It also pings at low speeds when warmed up.  I did the test the book calls for(push up on the diaphram when its idling) and it worked ok.  I also felt the diaphram when giving it the throttle(no comments please monkeyman :-) )<BR>Any other thoughts before I do the "Converter Shuffle?".....||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||02-19-2000||10:21 PM||||Might wanna' try premium gasoline (93 around the parts I live). That should stop the ping, if it is detonation. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||02-19-2000||10:38 PM||||I agree with Fiero56 in that it seems you still have some problems with the EGR "system".  Welding isn't one of my favs, so I just had a local muffler shop install my cat. converter.  Cost for install was about $40(well worth it just for the time it saved me).  The design and construction of the Catco converter is exactly the same as that of the "name brand" converters for approx. half the cost.  Although the dual bed catalysts can slightly reduce NOx emmission, they are still intended to act primarily on CO and HC components.  The reason for suggesting that you replace the cat is for performance concerns.   Many cats will partially (or even totally) clog as they melt or exceed their lifespan.  This clogging may occur over an extended period of time which makes the resulting loss of performance difficult to detect.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000005||tiacPaul||02-19-2000||11:37 PM||||My 84 duke failed even though I use premium shell gas, replaced a lot of sensors, and put in a new engine from an 86 celeb.  Any ideas?  They gave me the one year deal because I spent so damn much on the thing.  Any more ideas?||||reg||5
Z||000006||mwbackus||02-20-2000||01:35 AM||||Sure, if everything else is in good conditon (ie.. cat. converter, O2 sensor, and EGR system):<P>1)  A tune-up (proper ignition function is a must)<P>2)  Change the oil, oil filter, and air filter(if needed).<P>3)  Dump approx. 5 bottles of gas line antifreeze(dryer)in with 1/4 tank of fuel right before the test.  Important Note:  Completly fill the tank with fuel "promptly" after the test!!<P>Mike<P><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 02-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||02-20-2000||06:23 AM||||I would try going to a different inspection station as well. Did this with my Tercel. It failed at one garage and passed 3 days later at another and I didn't change a thing!!||||reg||2
Z||000008||Robert||02-20-2000||08:42 PM|||| I went though major trauma trying to get my 87GT to pass smog.  I finally gave up and had a new cat put in.  When I asked the guy at my local muffler shop for a “free flowing” cat, his eyes rolled up and said he must get that request 10 times a week, although usually from his much younger drivers.  He told me that all replacement cats are “free flowing”.  <BR>When I then took my car back to the smog shop, it still didn’t pass smog, the technician looked under my car and ask me “it this a new cat?”.  I told him it was about an hour old, so he proceeded to rev the engine up for about 15 minutes, ran the test again, and it passed. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||02-21-2000||11:35 AM||||Changing your cat will not make any difference in you NOx. I too had NOx issues.. with a properly working EGR system it passed. Mine were MUCH higher than yours.. NOx is caused by INSANLY high combustion temps... the only way to fix that is to dilute it with the EGR... I'll go back and look for my EGR issue post and get back to you.. but the cat has NO baring on the NOx. You can change it and get better CO and HC but not NOx. Not all cats are "free flowing" yes they are better flowing than an old crappy one but not exactly freeer (that a word?) Through Random Technology and Engineered Performance ( <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  )they can get you a better cat. but the cat only works when its hot! REALLY hot. more to follow... this may take a while to load sorry.. still haven't figured out this new computer...  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/emission.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/emission.jpg</A>  Ok just got off the phone with the shop.. a to hot a plug, malfunctioning EGR system and lack of fuel can all cause high NOx. but since your so close to failing HC and CO the fuel shouldn't be an issue. So colder plugs and check for vaccum leaks for the EGR to kinda work but not enough... <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||02-21-2000||07:38 PM||||Sorry, but your statement that catalytic converters have absolutely NO effect on NOx emmision is incorrect.  Rhodium and Iridium are components of the catalyst substrate of "dual" bed converters, and act to eliminate oxides of nitrogen.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000011||Brian84SE||02-21-2000||08:37 PM||||Thanks guys for your input.  Now here is something else to chew on: <BR>The engine has been running rather warm since I got it. (220-230). I just "burped" it tonite and the temperature went down considerably. Could a hot engine cause hot combustion temperatures? I checked the egr again using recommended procedures.||||reg||1
